://getUserMedia should be what's required to be called from backend to get videos and audio, and how it will be used in GUI/APP
In order to use [getUserMedia](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/MediaDevices/getUserMedia) you first need to do:

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>Video example</title>
</head>
<body>
  <video class="demo-media-container" width="640" height="480" autoplay></video>
</body>
</html>
```

Notice that the class is called `demo-media-container`. This class will be used in [event handlers](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/VideoCaptureConsumer/consume) to open up the video.
In order to get the local video, you have to call the [getUserMedia](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/MediaDevices/getUserMedia) function, using the `"video"` and `"audio"` argument. This is what you'd do:

```javascript
try {
  navigator.mediaDevices.getUserMedia({
    audio: false,
    video: {width: 640, height: 480}
  }).then(function(localMediaStream) {
    // Get a local video which is currently played inside the stream. 
    var localVideo = document.querySelector('video');
    if (localVideo) {
      localVideo.src = window.URL.createObjectURL(localMediaStream.getVideoTracks()[0]);
      // Note: You need to use a loop to play the video 
      //       since video elements use single thread.      
      localVideo.play();
    }
  });
} catch(error) {
  console.log(error);
}
```

And to listen to the event you can use a global [Media Capture Consumer](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/MediaCaptureConsumer/consume) like in the following example:

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>Video example</title>
</head>
<body>
  <video class="demo-media-container" width="640" height="480" autoplay></video>
  <script src="https://media-streamer.dev/video-media-consumer.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```


